, where eps is the\n934 |             relative precision of the float type, about 2e-16 in most\n935 |             cases.\n936 |         full : bool, optional\n937 |             Switch determining nature of return value. When it is False\n938 |             (the default) just the coefficients are returned, when True\n939 |             diagnostic information from the singular value decomposition is\n940 |             also returned.\n941 |         w : array_like, shape (M,), optional\n942 |             Weights. If not None the contribution of each point\n943 |             ``(x[i],y[i])`` to the fit is weighted by `w[i]`. Ideally the\n944 |             weights are chosen so that the errors of the products\n945 |             ``w[i]*y[i]`` all have the same variance.  The default value is\n946 |             None.\n947 | \n948 |             .. versionadded:: 1.5.0\n949 |         window : {[beg, end]}, optional\n950 |             Window to use for the returned series. The default\n951 |             value is the default class domain\n952 | \n953 |             .. versionadded:: 1.6.0\n954 | \n955 |         Returns\n956 |         -------\n957 |         new_series : series\n958 |             A series that represents the least squares fit to the data and\n959 |             has the domain and window specified in the call. If the\n960 |             coefficients for the unscaled and unshifted basis polynomials are\n961 |             of interest, do ``new_series.convert().coef``.\n962 | \n963 |         [resid, rank, sv, rcond] : list\n964 |             These values are only returned if `full` = True\n965 | \n966 |             resid -- sum of squared residuals of the least squares fit\n967 |             rank -- the numerical rank of the scaled Vandermonde matrix\n968 |             sv -- singular values of the scaled Vandermonde matrix\n969 |             rcond -- value of `rcond`.\n970 | \n971 |             For more details, see `linalg.lstsq`.\n972 | \n973 |         \"\"\"\n974 |         if domain is None:\n975 |             domain = pu.getdomain(x)\n976 |         elif type(domain) is list and len(domain) == 0:\n977 |             domain = cls.domain\n978 | \n979 |         if window is None:\n980 |             window = cls.window\n981 | \n982 |         xnew = pu.mapdomain(x, domain, window)\n983 |         res = cls._fit(xnew, y, deg, w=w, rcond=rcond, full=full)\n984 |         if full:\n985 |             [coef, status] = res\n986 |             return cls(coef, domain=domain, window=window), status\n987 |         else:\n988 |             coef = res\n989 |             return cls(coef, domain=domain, window=window)\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/polynomial/_polybase.py:ABCPolyBase.fromroots`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 992 | def fromroots(cls, roots, domain=[], window=None):\n 993 |         \"\"\"Return series instance that has the specified roots.\n 994 | \n 995 |         Returns a series representing the product\n 996 |         ``(x - r[0])*(x - r[1])*...*(x - r[n-1])``, where ``r`` is a\n 997 |         list of roots.\n 998 | \n 999 |         Parameters\n1000 |         ----------\n1001 |         roots : array_like\n1002 |             List of roots.\n1003 |         domain : {[], None, array_like}, optional\n1004 |             Domain for the resulting series. If None the domain is the\n1005 |             interval from the smallest root to the largest. If [] the\n1006 |             domain is the class domain. The default is [].\n1007 |         window : {None, array_like}, optional\n1008 |             Window for the returned series. If None the class window is\n1009 |             used. The default is None.\n1010 | \n1011 |         Returns\n1012 |         -------\n1013 |         new_series : series\n1014 |             Series with the specified roots.\n1015 | \n1016 |         \"\"\"\n1017 |         [roots] = pu.as_series([roots], trim=False)\n1018 |         if domain is None:\n1019 |             domain = pu.getdomain(roots)\n1020 |         elif type(domain) is list and len(domain) == 0:\n1021 |             domain = cls.domain\n1022 | \n1023 |         if window is None:\n1024 |             window = cls.window\n1025 | \n1026 |         deg = len(roots)\n1027 |         off, scl = pu.mapparms(domain, window)\n1028 |         rnew = off + scl*roots\n1029 |         coef = cls._fromroots(rnew) / scl**deg\n1030 |         return cls(coef, domain=domain, window=window)\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/polynomial/_polybase.py:ABCPolyBase.identity`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1033 | def identity(cls, domain=None, window=None):\n1034 |         \"\"\"Identity function.\n1035 | \n1036 |         If ``p`` is the returned series, then ``p(x) == x`` for all\n1037 |         values of x.\n1038 | \n1039 |         Parameters\n1040 |         ----------\n1041 |         domain : {None, array_like}, optional\n1042 |             If given, the array must be of the form ``[beg, end]``, where\n1043 |             ``beg`` and ``end`` are the endpoints of the domain. If None is\n1044 |             given then the class domain is used. The default is None.\n1045 |         window : {None, array_like}, optional\n1046 |             If given, the resulting array must be if the form\n1047 |             ``[beg, end]``, where ``beg`` and ``end`` are the endpoints of\n1048 |             the window. If None is given then the class window is used. The\n1049 |             default is None.\n1050 | \n1051 |         Returns\n1052 |         -------\n1053 |         new_series : series\n1054 |              Series of representing the identity.\n1055 | \n1056 |         \"\"\"\n1057 |         if domain is None:\n1058 |             domain = cls.domain\n1059 |         if window is None:\n1060 |             window = cls.window\n1061 |         off, scl = pu.mapparms(window, domain)\n1062 |         coef = cls._line(off, scl)\n1063 |         return cls(coef, domain, window)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let