`.\n 60 | \n 61 |     Parameters\n 62 |     ----------\n 63 |     n_samples : int, default=100\n 64 |         The number of samples.\n 65 | \n 66 |     n_features : int, default=20\n 67 |         The total number of features. These comprise ``n_informative``\n 68 |         informative features, ``n_redundant`` redundant features,\n 69 |         ``n_repeated`` duplicated features and\n 70 |         ``n_features-n_informative-n_redundant-n_repeated`` useless features\n 71 |         drawn at random.\n 72 | \n 73 |     n_informative : int, default=2\n 74 |         The number of informative features. Each class is composed of a number\n 75 |         of gaussian clusters each located around the vertices of a hypercube\n 76 |         in a subspace of dimension ``n_informative``. For each cluster,\n 77 |         informative features are drawn independently from  N(0, 1) and then\n 78 |         randomly linearly combined within each cluster in order to add\n 79 |         covariance. The clusters are then placed on the vertices of the\n 80 |         hypercube.\n 81 | \n 82 |     n_redundant : int, default=2\n 83 |         The number of redundant features. These features are generated as\n 84 |         random linear combinations of the informative features.\n 85 | \n 86 |     n_repeated : int, default=0\n 87 |         The number of duplicated features, drawn randomly from the informative\n 88 |         and the redundant features.\n 89 | \n 90 |     n_classes : int, default=2\n 91 |         The number of classes (or labels) of the classification problem.\n 92 | \n 93 |     n_clusters_per_class : int, default=2\n 94 |         The number of clusters per class.\n 95 | \n 96 |     weights : array-like of shape (n_classes,) or (n_classes - 1,),\\\n 97 |               default=None\n 98 |         The proportions of samples assigned to each class. If None, then\n 99 |         classes are balanced. Note that if ``len(weights) == n_classes - 1``,\n100 |         then the last class weight is automatically inferred.\n101 |         More than ``n_samples`` samples may be returned if the sum of\n102 |         ``weights`` exceeds 1.\n103 | \n104 |     flip_y : float, default=0.01\n105 |         The fraction of samples whose class is assigned randomly. Larger\n106 |         values introduce noise in the labels and make the classification\n107 |         task harder. Note that the default setting flip_y > 0 might lead\n108 |         to less than n_classes in y in some cases.\n109 | \n110 |     class_sep : float, default=1.0\n111 |         The factor multiplying the hypercube size.  Larger values spread\n112 |         out the clusters/classes and make the classification task easier.\n113 | \n114 |     hypercube : bool, default=True\n115 |         If True, the clusters are put on the vertices of a hypercube. If\n116 |         False, the clusters are put on the vertices of a random polytope.\n117 | \n118 |     shift : float, array of shape (n_features,) or None, default=0.0\n119 |         Shift features by the specified value. If None, then features\n120 |         are shifted by a random value drawn in [-class_sep, class_sep].\n121 | \n122 |     scale : float, array of shape (n_features,) or None, default=1.0\n123 |         Multiply features by the specified value. If None, then features\n124 |         are scaled by a random value drawn in [1, 100]. Note that scaling\n125 |         happens after shifting.\n126 | \n127 |     shuffle : bool, default=True\n128 |         Shuffle the samples and the features.\n129 | \n130 |     random_state : int or RandomState instance, default=None\n131 |         Determines random number generation for dataset creation. Pass an int\n132 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n133 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n134 | \n135 |     Returns\n136 |     -------\n137 |     X : array of shape [n_samples, n_features]\n138 |         The generated samples.\n139 | \n140 |     y : array of shape [n_samples]\n141 |         The integer labels for class membership of each sample.\n142 | \n143 |     Notes\n144 |     -----\n145 |     The algorithm is adapted from Guyon [1] and was designed to generate\n146 |     the \"Madelon\" dataset.\n147 | \n148 |     References\n149 |     ----------\n150 |     .. [1] I. Guyon, \"Design of experiments for the NIPS 2003 variable\n151 |            selection benchmark\", 2003.\n152 | \n153 |     See also\n154 |     --------\n155 |     make_blobs: simplified variant\n156 |     make_multilabel_classification: unrelated generator for multilabel tasks\n157 |     \"\"\"\n158 |     generator = check_random_state(random_state)\n159 | \n160 |     # Count features, clusters and samples\n161 |     if n_informative + n_redundant + n_repeated > n_features:\n162 |         raise ValueError(\"Number of informative, redundant and repeated \"\n163 |                          \"features must sum to less than the number of total\"\n164 |                          \" features\")\n165 |     # Use log2 to avoid overflow errors\n166 |     if n_informative < np.log2(n_classes * n_clusters_per_class):\n167 |         msg = \"n_classes({}) * n_clusters_per_class({}) must be\"\n168 |         msg += \" smaller or equal 2**n_informative({})={}\"\n169 |         raise ValueError(msg.format(n_classes, n_clusters_per_class,\n170 |                                     n_informative, 2**n_informative))\n171 | \n172 |     if weights is not None:\n173 |         if len(weights) not in [n_classes, n_classes - 1]:\n174 |             raise ValueError(\"Weights specified but incompatible with number \"\n175 |                              \"of classes.\")\n176 |         if len(weights) == n_classes - 1:\n177 |             if isinstance(weights, list):\n178 |                 weights = weights + [1.0 - sum(weights)]\n179 |             else:\n180 |                 weights = np.resize(weights, n_classes)\n181 |                 weights[-1] = 1.0 - sum(weights[:-1])\n182 |     else:\n183 |         weights = [1.0 / n_classes] * n_classes\n184 | \n185 |     n_useless = n_features - n_informative - n_redundant - n_repeated\n186 |     n_clusters = n_classes * n_clusters_per_class\n187 | \n188 |     # Distribute samples among clusters by weight\n189 |     n_samples_per_cluster = [\n190 |         int(n_samples * weights[k % n_classes] / n_clusters_per_class)\n191 |         for k in range(n_clusters)]\n192 | \n193 |     for i in range(n_samples - sum(n_samples_per_cluster)):\n194 |         n_samples_per_cluster[i % n_clusters] += 1\n195 | \n196 |     # Initialize X and y\n197 |     X = np.zeros((n_samples, n_features))\n198 |     y = np.zeros(n_samples, dtype=int)\n199 | \n200 |     # Build the polytope whose vertices become cluster centroids\n201 |     centroids = _generate_hypercube(n_clusters, n_informative,\n202 |                                     generator).astype(float, copy=False)\n203 |     centroids *= 2 * class_sep\n204 |     centroids -= class_sep\n205 |     if not hypercube:\n206 |         centroids *= generator.rand(n_clusters, 1)\n207 |         centroids *= generator.rand(1, n_informative)\n208 | \n209 |     # Initially draw informative features from the standard normal\n210 |     X[:, :n_informative] = generator.randn(n_samples, n_informative)\n211 | \n212 |     # Create each cluster; a variant of make_blobs\n213 |     stop = 0\n214 |     for k, centroid in enumerate(centroids):\n215 |         start, stop = stop, stop + n_samples_per_cluster[k]\n216 |         y[start:stop] = k % n_classes  # assign labels\n217 |         X_k = X[start:stop, :n_informative]  # slice a view of the cluster\n218 | \n219 |         A = 2 * generator.rand(n_informative, n_informative) - 1\n220 |         X_k[...] = np.dot(X_k, A)  # introduce random covariance\n221 | \n222 |         X_k += centroid  # shift the cluster to a vertex\n223 | \n224 |     # Create redundant features\n225 |     if n_redundant > 0:\n226 |         B = 2 * generator.rand(n_informative, n_redundant) - 1\n227 |         X[:, n_informative:n_informative + n_redundant] = \\\n228 |             np.dot(X[:, :n_informative], B)\n229 | \n230 |     # Repeat some features\n231 |     if n_repeated > 0:\n232 |         n = n_informative + n_redundant\n233 |         indices = ((n - 1) * generator.rand(n_repeated) + 0.5).astype(np.intp)\n234 |         X[:, n:n + n_repeated] = X[:, indices]\n235 | \n236 |     # Fill useless features\n237 |     if n_useless > 0:\n238 |         X[:, -n_useless:] = generator.randn(n_samples, n_useless)\n239 | \n240 |     # Randomly replace labels\n241 |     if flip_y >= 0.0:\n242 |         flip_mask = generator.rand(n_samples) < flip_y\n243 |         y[flip_mask] = generator.randint(n_classes, size=flip_mask.sum())\n244 | \n245 |     # Randomly shift and scale\n246 |     if shift is None:\n247 |         shift = (2 * generator.rand(n_features) - 1) * class_sep\n248 |     X += shift\n249 | \n250 |     if scale is None:\n251 |         scale = 1 + 100 * generator.rand(n_features)\n252 |     X *= scale\n253 | \n254 |     if shuffle:\n255 |         # Randomly permute samples\n256 |         X, y = util_shuffle(X, y, random_state=generator)\n257 | \n258 |         # Randomly permute features\n259 |         indices = np.arange(n_features)\n260 |         generator.shuffle(indices)\n261 |         X[:, :] = X[:, indices]\n262 | \n263 |     return X, y\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n430 | def _get_data_description_by_id(\n431 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n432 | ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n433 |     # OpenML API function: https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_id\n434 |     url = _DATA_INFO.format(data_id)\n435 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n436 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n437 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n438 |     )\n439 |    