eved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 40 | XY_METADATA = (\n 41 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 42 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976069',\n 43 |         checksum=('ed40f7e418d10484091b059703eeb95a'\n 44 |                   'e3199fe042891dcec4be6696b9968374'),\n 45 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt0.dat.gz'),\n 46 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 47 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976066',\n 48 |         checksum=('87700668ae45d45d5ca1ef6ae9bd81ab'\n 49 |                   '0f5ec88cc95dcef9ae7838f727a13aa6'),\n 50 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt1.dat.gz'),\n 51 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 52 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976063',\n 53 |         checksum=('48143ac703cbe33299f7ae9f4995db4'\n 54 |                   '9a258690f60e5debbff8995c34841c7f5'),\n 55 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt2.dat.gz'),\n 56 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 57 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976060',\n 58 |         checksum=('dfcb0d658311481523c6e6ca0c3f5a3'\n 59 |                   'e1d3d12cde5d7a8ce629a9006ec7dbb39'),\n 60 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt3.dat.gz'),\n 61 |     RemoteFileMetadata(\n 62 |         url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976057',\n 63 |         checksum=('5468f656d0ba7a83afc7ad44841cf9a5'\n 64 |                   '3048a5c083eedc005dcdb5cc768924ae'),\n 65 |         filename='lyrl2004_vectors_train.dat.gz')\n 66 | )\n 67 | \n 68 | # The original data can be found at:\n 69 | # http://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a08-topic-qrels/rcv1-v2.topics.qrels.gz\n 70 | TOPICS_METADATA = RemoteFileMetadata(\n 71 |     url='https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/5976048',\n 72 |     checksum=('2a98e5e5d8b770bded93afc8930d882'\n 73 |               '99474317fe14181aee1466cc754d0d1c1'),\n 74 |     filename='rcv1v2.topics.qrels.gz')\n 75 | \n 76 | logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py:fetch_rcv1`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 80 | def fetch_rcv1(*, data_home=None, subset='all', download_if_missing=True,\n 81 |                random_state=None, shuffle=False, return_X_y=False):\n 82 |     \"\"\"Load the RCV1 multilabel dataset (classification).\n 83 | \n 84 |     Download it if necessary.\n 85 | \n 86 |     Version: RCV1-v2, vectors, full sets, topics multilabels.\n 87 | \n 88 |     =================   =====================\n 89 |     Classes                               103\n 90 |     Samples total                      804414\n 91 |     Dimensionality                      47236\n 92 |     Features            real, between 0 and 1\n 93 |     =================   =====================\n 94 | \n 95 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <rcv1_dataset>`.\n 96 | \n 97 |     .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 98 | \n 99 |     Parameters\n100 |     ----------\n101 |     data_home : str, default=None\n102 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the datasets. By default\n103 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n104 | \n105 |     subset : {'train', 'test', 'all'}, default='all'\n106 |         Select the dataset to load: 'train' for the training set\n107 |         (23149 samples), 'test' for the test set (781265 samples),\n108 |         'all' for both, with the training samples first if shuffle is False.\n109 |         This follows the official LYRL2004 chronological split.\n110 | \n111 |     download_if_missing : bool, default=True\n112 |         If False, raise a IOError if the data is not locally available\n113 |         instead of trying to download the data from the source site.\n114 | \n115 |     random_state : int or RandomState instance, default=None\n116 |         Determines random number generation for dataset shuffling. Pass an int\n117 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n118 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n119 | \n120 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n121 |         Whether to shuffle dataset.\n122 | \n123 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n124 |         If True, returns ``(dataset.data, dataset.target)`` instead of a Bunch\n125 |         object. See below for more information about the `dataset.data` and\n126 |         `dataset.target` object.\n127 | \n128 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n129 | \n130 |     Returns\n131 |     -------\n132 |     dataset : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n133 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n134 | \n135 |         data : scipy csr array, dtype np.float64, shape (804414, 47236)\n136 |             The array has 0.16% of non zero values.\n137 |         target : scipy csr array, dtype np.uint8, shape (804414, 103)\n138 |             Each sample has a value of 1 in its categories, and 0 in others.\n139 |             The array has 3.15% of non zero values.\n140 |         sample_id : numpy array, dtype np.uint32, shape (804414,)\n141 |             Identification number of each sample, as ordered in dataset.data.\n142 |         target_names : numpy array, dtype object, length (103)\n143 |             Names of each target (RCV1 topics), as ordered in dataset.target.\n144 |         DESCR : string\n145 |             Description of the RCV1 dataset.\n146 | \n147 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n148 | \n149 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n150 |     \"\"\"\n151 |     N_SAMPLES = 804414\n152 |     N_FEATURES = 47236\n153 |     N_CATEGORIES = 103\n154 |     N_TRAIN = 23149\n155 | \n156 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n157 |     rcv1_dir = join(data_home, \"RCV1\")\n158 |     if download_if_missing:\n159 |         if not exists(rcv1_dir):\n160 |             makedirs(rcv1_dir)\n161 | \n162 |     samples_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"samples.pkl\")\n163 |     sample_id_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_id.pkl\")\n164 |     sample_topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"sample_topics.pkl\")\n165 |     topics_path = _pkl_filepath(rcv1_dir, \"topics_names.pkl\")\n166 | \n167 |     # load data (X) and sample_id\n168 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(samples_path) or\n169 |                                 not exists(sample_id_path)):\n170 |         files = []\n171 |         for each in XY_METADATA:\n172 |             logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % each.url)\n173 |             file_path = _fetch_remote(each, dirname=rcv1_dir)\n174 |             files.append(GzipFile(filename=file_path))\n175 | \n176 |         Xy = load_svmlight_files(files, n_features=N_FEATURES)\n177 | \n178 |         # Training data is before testing data\n179 |         X = sp.vstack([Xy[8], Xy[0], Xy[2], Xy[4], Xy[6]]).tocsr()\n180 |         sample_id = np.hstack((Xy[9], Xy[1], Xy[3], Xy[5], Xy[7]))\n181 |         sample_id = sample_id.astype(np.uint32, copy=False)\n182 | \n183 |         joblib.dump(X, samples_path, compress=9)\n184 |         joblib.dump(sample_id, sample_id_path, compress=9)\n185 | \n186 |         # delete archives\n187 |         for f in files:\n188 |             f.close()\n189 |             remove(f.name)\n190 |     else:\n191 |         X = joblib.load(samples_path)\n192 |         sample_id = joblib.load(sample_id_path)\n193 | \n194 |     # load target (y), categories, and sample_id_bis\n195 |     if download_if_missing and (not exists(sample_topics_path) or\n196 |                                 not exists(topics_path)):\n197 |         logger.info(\"Downloading %s\" % TOPICS_METADATA.url)\n198 |         topics_archive_path = _fetch_remote(TOPICS_METADATA,\n199 |                                             dirname=rcv1_dir)\n200 | \n201 |         # parse the target file\n202 |         n_cat = -1\n203 |         n_doc = -1\n204 |         doc_previous = -1\n205 |         y = np.zeros((N_SAMPLES, N_CATEGORIES), dtype=np.uint8)\n206 |         sample_id_bis = np.zeros(N_SAMPLES, dtype=np.int32)\n207 |         category_names = {}\n208 |         with GzipFile(filename=topics_archive_path, mode='rb') as f:\n209 |             for line in f:\n210 |                 line_components = line.decode(\"ascii\").split(\" \")\n211 |                 if len(line_components) == 3:\n212 |                     cat, doc, _ = line_components\n213 |                     if cat not in category_names:\n214 |                         n_cat += 1\n215 |                         category_names[cat] = n_cat\n216 | \n217 |                     doc = int(doc)\n218 |                     if doc != doc_previous:\n219 |                         doc_previous = doc\n220 |                         n_doc += 1\n221 |                         sample_id_bis[n_doc] = doc\n222 |                     y[n_doc, category_names[cat]] = 1\n223 | \n224 |         # delete archive\n225 |         remove(topics_archive_path)\n226 | \n227 |         # Samples in X are ordered with sample_id,\n228 |         # whereas in y, they are ordered with sample_id_bis.\n229 |         permutation = _find_permutation(sample_id_bis, sample_id)\n230 |         y = y[permutation, :]\n231 | \n232 |         # save category names in a list, with same order than y\n233 |         categories = np.empty(N_CATEGORIES, dtype=object)\n234 |         for k in category_names.keys():\n235 |             categories[category_names[k]] = k\n236 | \n237 |         # reorder categories in lexicographic order\n238 |         order = np.argsort(categories)\n239 |         categories = categories[order]\n240 |         y = sp.csr_matrix(y[:, order])\n241 | \n242 |         joblib.dump(y, sample_topics_path, compress=9)\n243 |         joblib.dump(categories, topics_path, compress=9)\n244 |     else:\n245 |         y = joblib.load(sample_topics_path)\n246 |         categories = joblib.load(topics_path)\n247 | \n248 |     if subset == 'all':\n249 |         pass\n250 |     elif subset == 'train':\n251 |         X = X[:N_TRAIN, :]\n252 |         y = y[:N_TRAIN, :]\n253 |         sample_id = sample_id[:N_TRAIN]\n254 |     elif subset == 'test':\n255 |         X = X[N_TRAIN:, :]\n256 |         y = y[N_TRAIN:, :]\n257 |         sample_id = sample_id[N_TRAIN:]\n258 |     else:\n259 |         raise ValueError(\"Unknown subset parameter. Got '%s' instead of one\"\n260 |                          \" of ('all', 'train', test')\" % subset)\n261 | \n262 |     if shuffle:\n263 |         X, y, sample_id = shuffle_(X, y, sample_id, random_state=random_state)\n264 | \n265 |     module_path = dirname(__file__)\n266 |     with open(join(module_path, 'descr', 'rcv1.rst')) as rst_file:\n267 |         fdescr = rst_file.read()\n268 | \n269 |     if return_X_y:\n270 |         return X, y\n271 | \n272 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, sample_id=sample_id,\n273 |                  target_names=categories, DESCR=fdescr)\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_fit_params`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1344 | def _check_fit_params(X, fit_params, indices=None):\n1345 |     \"\"\"Check and validate the parameters passed during `fit`.\n1346 | \n1347 |     Parameters\n1348 |     ----------\n1349 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1350 |         Data array.\n1351 | \n1352 |     fit_params : dict\n1353 |         Dictionary containing the parameters passed at fit.\n1354 | \n1355 |     indices : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n1356 |         Indices to be selected if the parameter has the same size as `X`.\n1357 | \n1358 |     Returns\n1359 |     -------\n1360 |     fit_params_validated : dict\n1361 |         Validated parameters. We ensure that the values support indexing.\n1362 |     \"\"\"\n1363 |     from . import _safe_indexing\n1364 |     fit_params_validated = {}\n1365 |     for param_key, param_value in fit_params.items():\n1366 |         if (not _is_arraylike(param_value) or\n1367 |                 _num_samples(param_value) != _num_samples(X)):\n1368 |             # Non-indexable pass-through (for now for backward-compatibility).\n1369 |             # https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/15805\n1370 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = param_value\n1371 |         else:\n1372 |             # Any other fit_params should support indexing\n1373 |             # (e.g. for cross-validation).\n1374 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = _make_indexable(param_value)\n1375 |             fit_params_validated[param_key] = _safe_indexing(\n1376 |                 fit_params_validated[param_key], indices\n1377 |             )\n1378 | \n1379 |     return fit_params_validated\n```\n\nFound file `benchmarks/bench_glm.py`.\nFound file `benchmarks/bench_mnist.py`.\nFound file `benchmarks/bench_glmnet.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"data_description md5_checksum\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `sklearn/datasets/_rcv1.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"RCV1 dataset.\n  2 | \n  3 | The dataset page is available at\n  4 | \n  5 |     http://jmlr.csail.mit.edu/papers/volume5/lewis04a/\n  6 | \"\"\"\n  7 | \n  8 | # Author: Tom Dupre la Tour\n  9 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 10 | \n 11 | import logging\n 12 | \n 13 | from os import remove, makedirs\n 14 | from os.path import dirname, exists, join\n 15 | from gzip import GzipFile\n 16 | \n 17 | import numpy as np\n 18 | import scipy.sparse as sp\n 19 | import joblib\n 20 | \n 21 | from . import get_data_home\n 22 | from ._base import _pkl_filepath\n 23 | from ._base import _fetch_remote\n 24 | from ._base import RemoteFileMetadata\n 25 | from ._svmlight_format_io import load_svmlight_files\n 26 | from ..utils import shuffle as shuffle_\n 27 | from ..utils import Bunch\n 28 | from ..utils.validation import _deprecate_positional_args\n 29 | \n 30 | \n 31 | # The original vectorized data can be found at:\n 32 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt0.dat.gz\n 33 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt1.dat.gz\n 34 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt2.dat.gz\n 35 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_test_pt3.dat.gz\n 36 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/a13-vector-files/lyrl2004_vectors_train.dat.gz\n 37 | # while the original stemmed token files can be found\n 38 | # in the README, section B.12.i.:\n 39 | #    http://www.ai.mit.edu/projects/jmlr/papers/volume5/lewis04a/lyrl2004_rcv1v2_README.htm\n```\n\nFound function `benchmarks/bench_mnist.py:load_data`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n61 | def load_data(dtype=np.float32, order='F'):\n62 |     \"\"\"Load the data, then cache and memmap the train/test split\"\"\"\n63 |     ######################################################################\n64 |     # Load dataset\n65 |     print(\"Loading dataset...\")\n66 |     data = fetch_openml('mnist_784')\n67 |     X = check_array(data['data'], dtype=dtype, order=order)\n68 |     y = data[\"target\"]\n69 | \n70 |     # Normalize features\n71 |     X = X / 255\n72 | \n73 |     # Create train-test split (as [Joachims, 2006])\n74 |     print(\"Creating train-test split...\")\n75 |     n_train = 60000\n76 |     X_train = X[:n_train]\n77 |     y_train = y[:n_train]\n78 |     X_test = X[n_train:]\n79 |     y_test = y[n_train:]\n80 | \n81 |     return X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test\n```\n\nFound function `benchmarks/bench_covertype.py:load_data`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n71 | def load_data(dtype=np.float32, order='C', random_state=13):\n72 |     \"\"\"Load the data, then cache and memmap the train/test split\"\"\"\n73 |     ######################################################################\n74 |     # Load dataset\n75 |     print(\"Loading dataset...\")\n76 |     data = fetch_covtype(download_if_missing=True, shuffle=True,\n77 |                          random_state=random_state)\n78 |     X = check_array(data['data'], dtype=dtype, order=order)\n79 |     y = (data['target'] != 1).astype(int)\n80 | \n81 |     # Create train-test split (as [Joachims, 2006])\n82 |     print(\"Creating train-test split...\")\n83 |     n_train = 522911\n84 |     X_train = X[:n_train]\n85 |     y_train = y[:n_train]\n86 |     X_test = X[n_train:]\n87 |     y_test = y[n_train:]\n88 | \n89 |     # Standardize first 10 features (the numerical ones)\n90 |     mean = X_train.mean(axis=0)\n91 |     std = X_train.std(axis=0)\n92 |     mean[10:] = 0.0\n93 |     std[10:] = 1.0\n94 |     X_train = (X_train - mean) / std\n95 |     X_test = (X_test - mean) / std\n96 |     return X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me analyze the key parts of the code that are relevant to implementing MD5 checksum validation:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id', 'sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response', 'sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_get_data_description_by_id`.\n```\n430 | def _get_data_description_by_id(\n431 |     data_id: int, data_home: Optional[str]\n432 | ) -> Dict[str, Any]:\n433 |     # OpenML API function: https://www.openml.org/api_docs#!/data/get_data_id\n434 |     url = _DATA_INFO.format(data_id)\n435 |     error_message = \"Dataset with data_id {} not found.\".format(data_id)\n436 |     json_data = _get_json_content_from_openml_api(\n437 |         url, error_message, data_home=data_home\n438 |     )\n439 |     return json_data['data_set_description']\n```\n\n\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n\n\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\n```\n509 | def _download_data_to_bunch(\n510 |     url: str,\n511 |     sparse: bool,\n512 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n513 |     *,\n514 |     as_frame: bool,\n515 |     features_list: List,\n516 |     data_columns: List[int],\n517 |     target_columns: List,\n518 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n519 | ):\n520 |     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n521 |     \"\"\"\n522 |     # NB: this function is long in order to handle retry for any failure\n523 |     #     during the streaming parse of the ARFF.\n524 | \n525 |     # Prepare which columns and data types should be returned for the X and y\n526 |     features_dict = {feature['name']: feature for feature in features_list}\n527 | \n528 |     # XXX: col_slice_y should be all nominal or all numeric\n529 |     _verify_target_data_type(features_dict, target_columns)\n530 | \n531 |     col_slice_y = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n532 |                    for col_name in target_columns]\n533 | \n534 |     col_slice_x = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n535 |                    for col_name in data_columns]\n536 |     for col_idx in col_slice_y:\n537 |         feat = features_list[col_idx]\n538 |         nr_missing = int(feat['number_of_missing_values'])\n539 |         if nr_missing > 0:\n540 |             raise ValueError('Target column {} has {} missing values. '\n541 |                              'Missing values are not supported for target '\n542 |                              'columns. '.format(feat['name'], nr_missing))\n543 | \n544 |     # Access an ARFF file on the OpenML server. Documentation:\n545 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_data_docs#!/data/get_download_id\n546 | \n547 |     if sparse is True:\n548 |         return_type = _arff.COO\n549 |     else:\n550 |         return_type = _arff.DENSE_GEN\n551 | \n552 |     frame = nominal_attributes = None\n553 | \n554 |     parse_arff: Callable\n555 |     postprocess: Callable\n556 |     if as_frame:\n557 |         columns = data_columns + target_columns\n558 |         parse_arff = partial(_convert_arff_data_dataframe, columns=columns,\n559 |                              features_dict=features_dict)\n560 | \n561 |         def postprocess(frame):\n562 |             X = frame[data_columns]\n563 |             if len(target_columns) >= 2:\n564 |                 y = frame[target_columns]\n565 |             elif len(target_columns) == 1:\n566 |                 y = frame[target_columns[0]]\n567 |             else:\n568 |                 y = None\n569 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n570 |     else:\n571 |         def parse_arff(arff):\n572 |             X, y = _convert_arff_data(arff, col_slice_x, col_slice_y, shape)\n573 |             # nominal attributes is a dict mapping from the attribute name to\n574 |             # the possible values. Includes also the target column (which will\n575 |             # be popped off below, before it will be packed in the Bunch\n576 |             # object)\n577 |             nominal_attributes = {k: v for k, v in arff['attributes']\n578 |                                   if isinstance(v, list) and\n579 |                                   k in data_columns + target_columns}\n580 |             return X, y, nominal_attributes\n581 | \n582 |         def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n583 |             is_classification = {col_name in nominal_attributes\n584 |                                  for col_name in target_columns}\n585 |             if not is_classification:\n586 |                 # No target\n587 |                 pass\n588 |             elif all(is_classification):\n589 |                 y = np.hstack([\n590 |                     np.take(\n591 |                         np.asarray(nominal_attributes.pop(col_name),\n592 |                                    dtype='O'),\n593 |                         y[:, i:i + 1].astype(int, copy=False))\n594 |                     for i, col_name in enumerate(target_columns)\n595 |                 ])\n596 |             elif any(is_classification):\n597 |                 raise ValueError('Mix of nominal and non-nominal targets is '\n598 |                                  'not currently supported')\n599 | \n600 |             # reshape y back to 1-D array, if there is only 1 target column;\n601 |             # back to None if there are not target columns\n602 |             if y.shape[1] == 1:\n603 |                 y = y.reshape((-1,))\n604 |             elif y.shape[1] == 0:\n605 |                 y = None\n606 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n607 | \n608 |     out = _retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)(\n609 |         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n610 |                              return_type=return_type,\n611 |                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n612 |                              parse_arff=parse_arff)\n613 |     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n614 | \n615 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n616 |                  categories=nominal_attributes,\n617 |                  feature_names=data_columns,\n618 |                  target_names=target_columns)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis of the code and the feature request, here are the key locations that need to be modified to implement MD5 checksum validation:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 430-439\nfunction: _get_data_description_by_id\nNotes: This function retrieves the dataset description from OpenML which includes the md5_checksum field\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 491-612\nfunction: _load_arff_response\nNotes: This function loads the ARFF data from the OpenML API response\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 509-615\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\nNotes: This function handles the actual download and data conversion process\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 881\nNotes: Location where the URL is constructed using data_description['file_id']\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 891-896\nNotes: Where the dataset description is updated with data from OpenML\n```\n\nThese locations are crucial because:\n1. `_get_data_description_by_id` is responsible for fetching the dataset metadata including the md5_checksum\n2. `_load_arff_response` is involved in loading the actual data\n3. `_download_data_to_bunch` handles the data download and conversion process\n4. The URL construction uses the file_id from data_description\n5. The dataset description update happens after data is loaded\n\nThe core of the implementation would need to:\n1. Add checksum validation logic in _download_data_to_bunch\n2. Utilize hashlib to compute the checksum of the downloaded data\n3. Compare computed checksum with the one from data_description\n4. Handle potential mismatches by raising warnings or errors\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 72463, "completion_tokens": 584}, "time": 94.45748710632324}]}}
