Expansions of CSR Matrices\n414 |             Using K-Simplex Numbers\" by Andrew Nystrom and John Hughes is\n415 |             used, which is much faster than the method used on CSC input. For\n416 |             this reason, a CSC input will be converted to CSR, and the output\n417 |             will be converted back to CSC prior to being returned, hence the\n418 |             preference of CSR.\n419 | \n420 |         Returns\n421 |         -------\n422 |         XP : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, NP)\n423 |             The matrix of features, where `NP` is the number of polynomial\n424 |             features generated from the combination of inputs. If a sparse\n425 |             matrix is provided, it will be converted into a sparse\n426 |             `csr_matrix`.\n427 |         \"\"\"\n428 |         check_is_fitted(self)\n429 | \n430 |         X = self._validate_data(\n431 |             X, order=\"F\", dtype=FLOAT_DTYPES, reset=False, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\")\n432 |         )\n433 | \n434 |         n_samples, n_features = X.shape\n435 |         max_int32 = np.iinfo(np.int32).max\n436 |         if sparse.isspmatrix_csr(X):\n437 |             if self._max_degree > 3:\n438 |                 return self.transform(X.tocsc()).tocsr()\n439 |             to_stack = []\n440 |             if self.include_bias:\n441 |                 to_stack.append(\n442 |                     sparse.csr_matrix(np.ones(shape=(n_samples, 1), dtype=X.dtype))\n443 |                 )\n444 |             if self._min_degree <= 1 and self._max_degree > 0:\n445 |                 to_stack.append(X)\n446 | \n447 |             cumulative_size = sum(mat.shape[1] for mat in to_stack)\n448 |             for deg in range(max(2, self._min_degree), self._max_degree + 1):\n449 |                 expanded = _create_expansion(\n450 |                     X=X,\n451 |                     interaction_only=self.interaction_only,\n452 |                     deg=deg,\n453 |                  