 of non-zero component in the random projection matrix in the\n228 |         range `(0, 1]`\n229 | \n230 |         If density = 'auto', the value is set to the minimum density\n231 |         as recommended by Ping Li et al.: 1 / sqrt(n_features).\n232 | \n233 |         Use density = 1 / 3.0 if you want to reproduce the results from\n234 |         Achlioptas, 2001.\n235 | \n236 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n237 |         Controls the pseudo random number generator used to generate the matrix\n238 |         at fit time.\n239 |         Pass an int for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n240 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n241 | \n242 |     Returns\n243 |     -------\n244 |     components : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_components, n_features)\n245 |         The generated Gaussian random matrix. Sparse matrix will be of CSR\n246 |         format.\n247 | \n248 |     See Also\n249 |     --------\n250 |     SparseRandomProjection\n251 | \n252 |     References\n253 |     ----------\n254 | \n255 |     .. [1] Ping Li, T. Hastie and K. W. Church, 2006,\n256 |            \"Very Sparse Random Projections\".\n257 |            https://web.stanford.edu/~hastie/Papers/Ping/KDD06_rp.pdf\n258 | \n259 |     .. [2] D. Achlioptas, 2001, \"Database-friendly random projections\",\n260 |            https://cgi.di.uoa.gr/~optas/papers/jl.pdf\n261 | \n262 |     \"\"\"\n263 |     _check_input_size(n_components, n_features)\n264 |     density = _check_density(density, n_features)\n265 |     rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n266 | \n267 |     if density == 1:\n268 |         # skip index generation if totally dense\n269 |         components = rng.binomial(1, 0.5, (n_components, n_features)) * 2 - 1\n270 |         return 1 / np.sqrt(n_components) * components\n271 | \n272 |     else:\n273 |         # Generate location of non zero elements\n274 |         indices = []\n275 |         offset = 0\n276 |         indptr = [offset]\n277 |         for _ in range(n_components):\n278 |             # find the indices of the non-zero components for row i\n279 |             n_nonzero_i = rng.binomial(n_features, density)\n280 |             indices_i = sample_without_replacement(\n281 |                 n_features, n_nonzero_i, random_state=rng\n282 |             )\n283 |             indices.append(indices_i)\n284 |             offset += n_nonzero_i\n285 |             indptr.append(offset)\n286 | \n287 |         indices = np.concatenate(indices)\n288 | \n289 |         # Among non zero components the probability of the sign is 50%/50%\n290 |         data = rng.binomial(1, 0.5, size=np.size(indices)) * 2 - 1\n291 | \n292 |         # build the CSR structure by concatenating the rows\n293 |         components = sp.csr_matrix(\n294 |             (data, indices, indptr), shape=(n_components, n_features)\n295 |         )\n296 | \n297 |         return np.sqrt(1 / density) / np.sqrt(n_components) * components\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_pairwise_distances_reduction/_dispatcher.py:BaseDistancesReductionDispatcher.is_usable_for.is_valid_sparse_matrix`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n107 | def is_valid_sparse_matrix(X):\n108 |             return (\n109 |                 isspmatrix_csr(X)\n110 |                 and\n111 |                 # TODO: support CSR matrices without non-zeros elements\n112 |                 X.nnz > 0\n113 |                 and\n114 |                 # TODO: support CSR matrices with int64 indices and indptr\n115 |                 # See: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/23653\n116 |                 X.indices.dtype == X.indptr.dtype == np.int32\n117 |             )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_samples_generator.py:make_sparse_spd_matrix`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1559 | def make_sparse_spd_matrix(\n1560 |     dim=1,\n1561 |     *,\n1562 |     alpha=0.95,\n1563 |     norm_diag=False,\n1564 |     smallest_coef=0.1,\n1565 |     largest_coef=0.9,\n1566 |     random_state=None,\n1567 | ):\n1568 |     \"\"\"Generate a sparse symmetric definite positive matrix.\n1569 | \n1570 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <sample_generators>`.\n1571 | \n1572 |     Parameters\n1573 |     ----------\n1574 |     dim : int, default=1\n1575 |         The size of the random matrix to generate.\n1576 | \n1577 |     alpha : float, default=0.95\n1578 |         The probability that a coefficient is zero (see notes). Larger values\n1579 |         enforce more sparsity. The value should be in the range 0 and 1.\n1580 | \n1581 |     norm_diag : bool, default=False\n1582 |         Whether to normalize the output matrix to make the leading diagonal\n1583 |         elements all 1.\n1584 | \n1585 |     smallest_coef : float, default=0.1\n1586 |         The value of the smallest coefficient between 0 and 1.\n1587 | \n1588 |     largest_coef : float, default=0.9\n1589 |         The value of the largest coefficient between 0 and 1.\n1590 | \n1591 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1592 |         Determines random number generation for dataset creation. Pass an int\n1593 |         for reproducible output across multiple function calls.\n1594 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1595 | \n1596 |     Returns\n1597 |     -------\n1598 |     prec : sparse matrix of shape (dim, dim)\n1599 |         The generated matrix.\n1600 | \n1601 |     See Also\n1602 |     --------\n1603 |     make_spd_matrix : Generate a random symmetric, positive-definite matrix.\n1604 | \n1605 |     Notes\n1606 |     -----\n1607 |     The sparsity is actually imposed on the cholesky factor of the matrix.\n1608 |     Thus alpha does not translate directly into the filling fraction of\n1609 |     the matrix itself.\n1610 |     \"\"\"\n1611 |     random_state = check_random_state(random_state)\n1612 | \n1613 |     chol = -np.eye(dim)\n1614 |     aux = random_state.uniform(size=(dim, dim))\n1615 |     aux[aux < alpha] = 0\n1616 |     aux[aux > alpha] = smallest_coef + (\n1617 |         largest_coef - smallest_coef\n1618 |     ) * random_state.uniform(size=np.sum(aux > alpha))\n1619 |     aux = np.tril(aux, k=-1)\n1620 | \n1621 |     # Permute the lines: we don't want to have asymmetries in the final\n1622 |     # SPD matrix\n1623 |     permutation = random_state.permutation(dim)\n1624 |     aux = aux[permutation].T[permutation]\n1625 |     chol += aux\n1626 |     prec = np.dot(chol.T, chol)\n1627 | \n1628 |     if norm_diag:\n1629 |         # Form the diagonal vector into a row matrix\n1630 |         d = np.diag(prec).reshape(1, prec.shape[0])\n1631 |         d = 1.0 / np.sqrt(d)\n1632 | \n1633 |         prec *= d\n1634 |         prec *= d.T\n1635 | \n1636 |     return prec\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the dependencies of SplineTransformer:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['TransformerMixin', 'BaseEstimator']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"TransformerMixin\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/base.py:TransformerMixin`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `TransformerMixin`.\n```\n864 | class TransformerMixin(_SetOutputMixin):\n865 |     \"\"\"Mixin class for all transformers in scikit-learn.\n866 | \n867 |     If :term:`get_feature_names_out` is defined, then `BaseEstimator` will\n868 |     automatically wrap `transform` and `fit_transform` to follow the `set_output`\n869 |     API. See the :ref:`developer_api_set_output` for details.\n870 | \n871 |     :class:`base.OneToOneFeatureMixin` and\n872 |     :class:`base.ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin` are helpful mixins for\n873 |     defining :term:`get_feature_names_out`.\n874 |     \"\"\"\n875 | \n876 |     def fit_transform(self, X, y=None, **fit_params):\n877 |         \"\"\"\n878 |         Fit to data, then transform it.\n879 | \n880 |         Fits transformer to `X` and `y` with optional parameters `fit_params`\n881 |         and returns a transformed version of `X`.\n882 | \n883 |         Parameters\n884 |         ----------\n885 |         X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n886 |             Input samples.\n887 | \n888 |         y :  array-like of shape (n_samples,) or (n_samples, n_outputs), \\\n889 |                 default=None\n890 |             Target values (None for unsupervised transformations).\n891 | \n892 |         **fit_params : dict\n893 |             Additional fit parameters.\n894 | \n895 |         Returns\n896 |         -------\n897 |         X_new : ndarray array of shape (n_samples, n_features_new)\n898 |             Transformed array.\n899 |         \"\"\"\n900 |         # non-optimized default implementation; override when a better\n901 |         # method is possible for a given clustering algorithm\n902 |         if y is None:\n903 |             # fit method of arity 1 (unsupervised transformation)\n904 |             return self.fit(X, **fit_params).transform(X)\n905 |         else:\n906 |             # fit method of arity 2 (supervised transformation)\n907 |             return self.fit(X, y, **fit_params).transform(X)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"BaseEstimator\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `BaseEstimator`.\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass BaseEstimator:\n\n    @classmethod\n    def _get_param_names(cls):\n...\n    def get_params(self, deep=True):\n...\n    def set_params(self, **params):\n...\n    def __sklearn_clone__(self):\n...\n    def __repr__(self, N_CHAR_MAX=700):\n...\n    def __getstate__(self):\n...\n    def __setstate__(self, state):\n...\n    def _more_tags(self):\n...\n    def _get_tags(self):\n...\n    def _check_n_features(self, X, reset):\n...\n    def _check_feature_names(self, X, *, reset):\n...\n    def _validate_data(\n        self,\n        X=\"no_validation\",\n        y=\"no_validation\",\n        reset=True,\n        validate_separately=False,\n        cast_to_ndarray=True,\n        **check_params,\n    ):\n...\n    def _validate_params(self):\n...\n    @property\n    def _repr_html_(self):\n...\n    def _repr_html_inner(self):\n...\n    def _repr_mimebundle_(self, **kwargs):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/base.py:BaseEstimator` to get the full content if needed.\n"}, {"content": "After analyzing all the search results and dependencies, I can confirm that the previously identified locations are complete and sufficient to implement the sparse matrix output feature for SplineTransformer. Here's why:\n\n1. The core SplineTransformer class in `sklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py` already has the infrastructure for sparse matrix handling through the `_csr_polynomial_expansion` module.\n\n2. The main methods that need modification are:\n   - `__init__` to add sparse parameter\n   - `fit` to handle sparse inputs\n   - `transform` to create sparse output\n   - `_create_expansion` for sparse matrix operations\n\n3. Key supporting components:\n   - The `_csr_polynomial_expansion` module shows how to properly handle sparse matrices in polynomial transformations\n   - The `BaseEstimator` and `TransformerMixin` classes provide the framework for proper sparse data handling\n   - The examples in `examples/linear_model/plot_polynomial_interpolation.py` demonstrate usage patterns\n\n4. No additional locations need to be included as:\n   - All required sparse matrix functionality is already implemented in the codebase\n   - The dependency analysis shows these are the key locations involved\n   - The implementation would follow the established pattern shown in PolynomialFeatures.transform\n\nHere's the location block in the required format:\n\n```\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 703-704\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: __init__\n\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 821-827\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: fit\n\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 943-950\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: transform\n\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 22-26\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: _create_expansion\n\nsklearn/preprocessing/_polynomial.py\nline: 1044-1050\nclass: SplineTransformer\nfunction: transform\n```\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 183930, "completion_tokens": 1240}, "time": 175.6820878982544}]}}
{"instance_id": "sympy__sympy-27325", "found_files": [["sympy/core/basic.py", "sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py", "sympy/functions/elementary/miscellaneous.py"]], "found_modules": [["sympy/parsing/autolev/_antlr/autolevparser.py:AutolevParser.OutputsContext", "sympy/polys/rings.py:PolyElement", "sympy/polys/rootisolation.py:ComplexInterval", "sympy/sets/sets.py:FiniteSet", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:TensExpr", "sympy/core/basic.py:Basic", "sympy/matrices/sparse.py:SparseRepMatrix", "sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:sign", "sympy/functions/special/error_functions.py:erfi", "sympy/physics/quantum/gate.py:IdentityGate", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:_TensorDataLazyEvaluator", "sympy/functions/elementary/miscellaneous.py:MinMaxBase"]], "found_entities": [["sympy/parsing/autolev/_antlr/autolevparser.py:AutolevParser.OutputsContext.Output", "sympy/polys/rings.py:PolyElement.max_norm", "sympy/polys/rootisolation.py:ComplexInterval.center", "sympy/polys/rings.py:PolyElement.l1_norm", "sympy/sets/sets.py:FiniteSet.__len__", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:TensExpr.__rpow__", "sympy/sets/sets.py:FiniteSet.as_relational", "sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite", "sympy/matrices/sparse.py:SparseRepMatrix.row_structure_symbolic_cholesky", "sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:sign._eval_rewrite_as_Abs", "sympy/functions/special/error_functions.py:erfi._eval_rewrite_as_expint", "sympy/physics/quantum/gate.py:IdentityGate._eval_commutator", "sympy/tensor/tensor.py:_TensorDataLazyEvaluator.__delitem__", "sympy/functions/elementary/miscellaneous.py:MinMaxBase._eval_rewrite_as_Abs"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, here are the key files and functions that need to be modified to implement the requested feature:\n\n```\nsympy/core/basic.py\nline: 2078-2089\nfunction: Basic.rewrite\n\nsympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py\nline: 439-440\nclass: sign\nfunction: _eval_rewrite_as_Abs\n\nsympy/functions/elementary/miscellaneous.py\nline: 643-647\nclass: MinMaxBase\nfunction: _eval_rewrite_as_Abs\n\nsympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py\nclass: Abs\n```\n\nThe core issue lies in the `rewrite` method in `sympy/core/basic.py`. Currently, it handles string rules by converting them to `_eval_rewrite_as_...` methods directly, but it doesn't have special handling for the built-in `abs` function. The fix would involve modifying how string rules are processed in the `rewrite` method to detect when `abs` is being used and then redirecting it to `Abs`-related rewrite rules.\n\nThe `sign` class already has proper `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` implementation, and other mathematical functions/classes would benefit from similar treatment. The `Abs` class is the primary target for these rewrite rules."], "meta_data": {"repo": "sympy/sympy", "base_commit": "fbe647fb34f90de01946fcaa1314207f11e5b26e", "problem_statement": "rewrite(abs) should call rewrite(Abs)\n```py\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(abs)\nsign(x)\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(Abs)\nPiecewise((0, Eq(x, 0)), (x/Abs(x), True))\n```\n\nThis is because `rewrite(x)` just dispatches to the method called `_eval_rewrite_as_x`, and the method here is called `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` since `Abs` is the name of the class. But most people use `abs`, the built-in function which dispatches to `Abs`. So it should be special-cased in rewrite to call `Abs`. \n", "patch": "diff --git a/sympy/core/basic.py b/sympy/core/basic.py\nindex 62ccd3ed5508..f9f04c728245 100644\n--- a/sympy/core/basic.py\n+++ b/sympy/core/basic.py\n@@ -2075,6 +2075,11 @@ def rewrite(self, *args, deep=True, **hints):\n         pattern = args[:-1]\n         rule = args[-1]\n \n+        # Special case: map `abs` to `Abs`\n+        if rule is abs:\n+            from sympy.functions.elementary.complexes import Abs\n+            rule = Abs\n+\n         # support old design by _eval_rewrite_as_[...] method\n         if isinstance(rule, str):\n             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % rule\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 29247, "completion_tokens": 497}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'sympy' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: rewrite(abs) should call rewrite(Abs)\n\n```py\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(abs)\nsign(x)\n>>> sign(x).rewrite(Abs)\nPiecewise((0, Eq(x, 0)), (x/Abs(x), True))\n```\n\nThis is because `rewrite(x)` just dispatches to the method called `_eval_rewrite_as_x`, and the method here is called `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` since `Abs` is the name of the class. But most people use `abs`, the built-in function which dispatches to `Abs`. So it should be special-cased in rewrite to call `Abs`.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n"}, {"content": "I'll help analyze and locate the relevant code areas for this feature request. Let me follow the steps systematically.\n\nStep 1: Let's first understand the key information:\n- Requested feature: Special-case handling of `rewrite(abs)` to call `rewrite(Abs)`\n- Core functionality: Rewrite dispatcher doesn't properly handle the built-in `abs` function\n- Related modules: Need to investigate rewrite functionality and absolute value handling\n\nLet me search for relevant code:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['rewrite', '_eval_rewrite_as_Abs', 'Abs']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"rewrite\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sympy/unify/rewrite.py`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `rewrite`.\n```\n 1 | \"\"\" Functions to support rewriting of SymPy expressions \"\"\"\n 2 | \n 3 | from sympy.core.expr import Expr\n 4 | from sympy.assumptions import ask\n 5 | from sympy.strategies.tools import subs\n 6 | from sympy.unify.usympy import rebuild, unify\n 7 | \n 8 | def rewriterule(source, target, variables=(), condition=None, assume=None):\n 9 |     \"\"\" Rewrite rule.\n10 | \n11 |     Transform expressions that match source into expressions that match target\n12 |     treating all ``variables`` as wilds.\n13 | \n14 |     Examples\n15 |     ========\n16 | \n17 |     >>> from sympy.abc import w, x, y, z\n18 |     >>> from sympy.unify.rewrite import rewriterule\n19 |     >>> from sympy import default_sort_key\n20 |     >>> rl = rewriterule(x + y, x**y, [x, y])\n21 |     >>> sorted(rl(z + 3), key=default_sort_key)\n22 |     [3**z, z**3]\n23 | \n24 |     Use ``condition`` to specify additional requirements.  Inputs are taken in\n25 |     the same order as is found in variables.\n26 | \n27 |     >>> rl = rewriterule(x + y, x**y, [x, y], lambda x, y: x.is_integer)\n28 |     >>> list(rl(z + 3))\n29 |     [3**z]\n30 | \n31 |     Use ``assume`` to specify additional requirements using new assumptions.\n32 | \n33 |     >>> from sympy.assumptions import Q\n34 |     >>> rl = rewriterule(x + y, x**y, [x, y], assume=Q.integer(x))\n35 |     >>> list(rl(z + 3))\n36 |     [3**z]\n37 | \n38 |     Assumptions for the local context are provided at rule runtime\n39 | \n40 |     >>> list(rl(w + z, Q.integer(z)))\n41 |     [z**w]\n42 |     \"\"\"\n43 | \n44 |     def rewrite_rl(expr, assumptions=True):\n45 |         for match in unify(source, expr, {}, variables=variables):\n46 |             if (condition and\n47 |                 not condition(*[match.get(var, var) for var in variables])):\n48 |                 continue\n49 |             if (assume and not ask(assume.xreplace(match), assumptions)):\n50 |                 continue\n51 |             expr2 = subs(match)(target)\n52 |             if isinstance(expr2, Expr):\n53 |                 expr2 = rebuild(expr2)\n54 |             yield expr2\n55 |     return rewrite_rl\n56 | \n```\n\nFound function `sympy/series/gruntz.py:rewrite`.\nFound function `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`.\nFound function `sympy/combinatorics/fp_groups.py:rewrite`.\nFound function `sympy/functions/elementary/piecewise.py:Piecewise._eval_rewrite_as_KroneckerDelta.rewrite`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `rewrite`.\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"_eval_rewrite_as_Abs\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:sign._eval_rewrite_as_Abs`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs`.\n```\n439 | def _eval_rewrite_as_Abs(self, arg, **kwargs):\n440 |         return Piecewise((0, Eq(arg, 0)), (arg / Abs(arg), True))\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/functions/elementary/miscellaneous.py:MinMaxBase._eval_rewrite_as_Abs`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs`.\n```\n643 | def _eval_rewrite_as_Abs(self, *args, **kwargs):\n644 |         from sympy.functions.elementary.complexes import Abs\n645 |         s = (args[0] + self.func(*args[1:]))/2\n646 |         d = abs(args[0] - self.func(*args[1:]))/2\n647 |         return (s + d if isinstance(self, Max) else s - d).rewrite(Abs)\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"Abs\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `Abs`.\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass Abs(DefinedFunction):\n\n    args: tTuple[Expr]\n\n    is_extended_real = True\n    is_extended_negative = False\n    is_extended_nonnegative = True\n    unbranched = True\n    _singularities = True  # non-holomorphic\n\n    def fdiff(self, argindex=1):\n...\n    @classmethod\n    def eval(cls, arg):\n...\n    def _eval_is_real(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_integer(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_extended_nonzero(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_zero(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_extended_positive(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_rational(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_even(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_odd(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_algebraic(self):\n...\n    def _eval_power(self, exponent):\n...\n    def _eval_nseries(self, x, n, logx, cdir=0):\n...\n    def _eval_derivative(self, x):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Heaviside(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Piecewise(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_sign(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_conjugate(self, arg, **kwargs):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sympy/plotting/intervalmath/lib_interval.py:Abs`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `Abs`.\n```\n 8 | def Abs(x):\n 9 |     if isinstance(x, (int, float)):\n10 |         return interval(abs(x))\n11 |     elif isinstance(x, interval):\n12 |         if x.start < 0 and x.end > 0:\n13 |             return interval(0, max(abs(x.start), abs(x.end)), is_valid=x.is_valid)\n14 |         else:\n15 |             return interval(abs(x.start), abs(x.end))\n16 |     else:\n17 |         raise NotImplementedError\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me get more details about the rewrite functionality:\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`.\n```\n1996 | def rewrite(self, *args, deep=True, **hints):\n1997 |         \"\"\"\n1998 |         Rewrite *self* using a defined rule.\n1999 | \n2000 |         Rewriting transforms an expression to another, which is mathematically\n2001 |         equivalent but structurally different. For example you can rewrite\n2002 |         trigonometric functions as complex exponentials or combinatorial\n2003 |         functions as gamma function.\n2004 | \n2005 |         This method takes a *pattern* and a *rule* as positional arguments.\n2006 |         *pattern* is optional parameter which defines the types of expressions\n2007 |         that will be transformed. If it is not passed, all possible expressions\n2008 |         will be rewritten. *rule* defines how the expression will be rewritten.\n2009 | \n2010 |         Parameters\n2011 |         ==========\n2012 | \n2013 |         args : Expr\n2014 |             A *rule*, or *pattern* and *rule*.\n2015 |             - *pattern* is a type or an iterable of types.\n2016 |             - *rule* can be any object.\n2017 | \n2018 |         deep : bool, optional\n2019 |             If ``True``, subexpressions are recursively transformed. Default is\n2020 |             ``True``.\n2021 | \n2022 |         Examples\n2023 |         ========\n2024 | \n2025 |         If *pattern* is unspecified, all possible expressions are transformed.\n2026 | \n2027 |         >>> from sympy import cos, sin, exp, I\n2028 |         >>> from sympy.abc import x\n2029 |         >>> expr = cos(x) + I*sin(x)\n2030 |         >>> expr.rewrite(exp)\n2031 |         exp(I*x)\n2032 | \n2033 |         Pattern can be a type or an iterable of types.\n2034 | \n2035 |         >>> expr.rewrite(sin, exp)\n2036 |         exp(I*x)/2 + cos(x) - exp(-I*x)/2\n2037 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos,], exp)\n2038 |         exp(I*x)/2 + I*sin(x) + exp(-I*x)/2\n2039 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos, sin], exp)\n2040 |         exp(I*x)\n2041 | \n2042 |         Rewriting behavior can be implemented by defining ``_eval_rewrite()``\n2043 |         method.\n2044 | \n2045 |         >>> from sympy import Expr, sqrt, pi\n2046 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2047 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite(self, rule, args, **hints):\n2048 |         ...         x, = args\n2049 |         ...         if rule == cos:\n2050 |         ...             return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2051 |         ...         if rule == sqrt:\n2052 |         ...             return sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2053 |         >>> MySin(MySin(x)).rewrite(cos)\n2054 |         cos(-cos(-x + pi/2) + pi/2)\n2055 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(sqrt)\n2056 |         sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2057 | \n2058 |         Defining ``_eval_rewrite_as_[...]()`` method is supported for backwards\n2059 |         compatibility reason. This may be removed in the future and using it is\n2060 |         discouraged.\n2061 | \n2062 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2063 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite_as_cos(self, *args, **hints):\n2064 |         ...         x, = args\n2065 |         ...         return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2066 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(cos)\n2067 |         cos(-x + pi/2)\n2068 | \n2069 |         \"\"\"\n2070 |         if not args:\n2071 |             return self\n2072 | \n2073 |         hints.update(deep=deep)\n2074 | \n2075 |         pattern = args[:-1]\n2076 |         rule = args[-1]\n2077 | \n2078 |         # support old design by _eval_rewrite_as_[...] method\n2079 |         if isinstance(rule, str):\n2080 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % rule\n2081 |         elif hasattr(rule, \"__name__\"):\n2082 |             # rule is class or function\n2083 |             clsname = rule.__name__\n2084 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2085 |         else:\n2086 |             # rule is instance\n2087 |             clsname = rule.__class__.__name__\n2088 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2089 | \n2090 |         if pattern:\n2091 |             if iterable(pattern[0]):\n2092 |                 pattern = pattern[0]\n2093 |             pattern = tuple(p for p in pattern if self.has(p))\n2094 |             if not pattern:\n2095 |                 return self\n2096 |         # hereafter, empty pattern is interpreted as all pattern.\n2097 | \n2098 |         return self._rewrite(pattern, rule, method, **hints)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check the usage of `abs` in SymPy:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['abs', 'Abs', 'rewrite']</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>sympy/**</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"abs\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sympy/polys/polytools.py:Poly.abs`.\nFound function `sympy/polys/polyclasses.py:DMP.abs`.\nFound function `sympy/polys/polyclasses.py:DMP_Python.abs`.\nFound function `sympy/polys/polyclasses.py:DUP_Flint.abs`.\nFound function `sympy/polys/domains/domain.py:Domain.abs`.\nFound function `sympy/parsing/latex/lark/transformer.py:TransformToSymPyExpr.abs`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `abs`.\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"Abs\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `Abs`.\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass Abs(DefinedFunction):\n\n    args: tTuple[Expr]\n\n    is_extended_real = True\n    is_extended_negative = False\n    is_extended_nonnegative = True\n    unbranched = True\n    _singularities = True  # non-holomorphic\n\n    def fdiff(self, argindex=1):\n...\n    @classmethod\n    def eval(cls, arg):\n...\n    def _eval_is_real(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_integer(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_extended_nonzero(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_zero(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_extended_positive(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_rational(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_even(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_odd(self):\n...\n    def _eval_is_algebraic(self):\n...\n    def _eval_power(self, exponent):\n...\n    def _eval_nseries(self, x, n, logx, cdir=0):\n...\n    def _eval_derivative(self, x):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Heaviside(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_Piecewise(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_sign(self, arg, **kwargs):\n...\n    def _eval_rewrite_as_conjugate(self, arg, **kwargs):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sympy/plotting/intervalmath/lib_interval.py:Abs`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `Abs`.\n```\n 8 | def Abs(x):\n 9 |     if isinstance(x, (int, float)):\n10 |         return interval(abs(x))\n11 |     elif isinstance(x, interval):\n12 |         if x.start < 0 and x.end > 0:\n13 |             return interval(0, max(abs(x.start), abs(x.end)), is_valid=x.is_valid)\n14 |         else:\n15 |             return interval(abs(x.start), abs(x.end))\n16 |     else:\n17 |         raise NotImplementedError\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"rewrite\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `sympy/unify/rewrite.py`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `rewrite`.\n```\n 1 | \"\"\" Functions to support rewriting of SymPy expressions \"\"\"\n 2 | \n 3 | from sympy.core.expr import Expr\n 4 | from sympy.assumptions import ask\n 5 | from sympy.strategies.tools import subs\n 6 | from sympy.unify.usympy import rebuild, unify\n 7 | \n 8 | def rewriterule(source, target, variables=(), condition=None, assume=None):\n 9 |     \"\"\" Rewrite rule.\n10 | \n11 |     Transform expressions that match source into expressions that match target\n12 |     treating all ``variables`` as wilds.\n13 | \n14 |     Examples\n15 |     ========\n16 | \n17 |     >>> from sympy.abc import w, x, y, z\n18 |     >>> from sympy.unify.rewrite import rewriterule\n19 |     >>> from sympy import default_sort_key\n20 |     >>> rl = rewriterule(x + y, x**y, [x, y])\n21 |     >>> sorted(rl(z + 3), key=default_sort_key)\n22 |     [3**z, z**3]\n23 | \n24 |     Use ``condition`` to specify additional requirements.  Inputs are taken in\n25 |     the same order as is found in variables.\n26 | \n27 |     >>> rl = rewriterule(x + y, x**y, [x, y], lambda x, y: x.is_integer)\n28 |     >>> list(rl(z + 3))\n29 |     [3**z]\n30 | \n31 |     Use ``assume`` to specify additional requirements using new assumptions.\n32 | \n33 |     >>> from sympy.assumptions import Q\n34 |     >>> rl = rewriterule(x + y, x**y, [x, y], assume=Q.integer(x))\n35 |     >>> list(rl(z + 3))\n36 |     [3**z]\n37 | \n38 |     Assumptions for the local context are provided at rule runtime\n39 | \n40 |     >>> list(rl(w + z, Q.integer(z)))\n41 |     [z**w]\n42 |     \"\"\"\n43 | \n44 |     def rewrite_rl(expr, assumptions=True):\n45 |         for match in unify(source, expr, {}, variables=variables):\n46 |             if (condition and\n47 |                 not condition(*[match.get(var, var) for var in variables])):\n48 |                 continue\n49 |             if (assume and not ask(assume.xreplace(match), assumptions)):\n50 |                 continue\n51 |             expr2 = subs(match)(target)\n52 |             if isinstance(expr2, Expr):\n53 |                 expr2 = rebuild(expr2)\n54 |             yield expr2\n55 |     return rewrite_rl\n56 | \n```\n\nFound function `sympy/series/gruntz.py:rewrite`.\nFound function `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`.\nFound function `sympy/combinatorics/fp_groups.py:rewrite`.\nFound function `sympy/functions/elementary/piecewise.py:Piecewise._eval_rewrite_as_KroneckerDelta.rewrite`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `rewrite`.\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n"}, {"content": "Based on my analysis, here are the key files and functions that need to be modified to implement the requested feature:\n\n```\nsympy/core/basic.py\nline: 2078-2089\nfunction: Basic.rewrite\n\nsympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py\nline: 439-440\nclass: sign\nfunction: _eval_rewrite_as_Abs\n\nsympy/functions/elementary/miscellaneous.py\nline: 643-647\nclass: MinMaxBase\nfunction: _eval_rewrite_as_Abs\n\nsympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py\nclass: Abs\n```\n\nThe core issue lies in the `rewrite` method in `sympy/core/basic.py`. Currently, it handles string rules by converting them to `_eval_rewrite_as_...` methods directly, but it doesn't have special handling for the built-in `abs` function. The fix would involve modifying how string rules are processed in the `rewrite` method to detect when `abs` is being used and then redirecting it to `Abs`-related rewrite rules.\n\nThe `sign` class already has proper `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs` implementation, and other mathematical functions/classes would benefit from similar treatment. The `Abs` class is the primary target for these rewrite rules.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 29247, "completion_tokens": 497}, "time": 62.78966307640076}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-28678", "found_files": [["sklearn/utils/validation.py", "sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py", "sklearn/base.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/feature_extraction/_dict_vectorizer.py:DictVectorizer", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_consistent_length", "sklearn/linear_model/_coordinate_descent.py:MultiTaskLassoCV", "sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_no_attributes_set_in_init", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:generate_valid_param", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:LegacyVersion", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:has_fit_parameter"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/feature_extraction/_dict_vectorizer.py:DictVectorizer._more_tags", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:check_consistent_length", "sklearn/linear_model/_coordinate_descent.py:MultiTaskLassoCV.fit", "sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_no_attributes_set_in_init", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:generate_valid_param", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:LegacyVersion.__repr__", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:has_fit_parameter"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on my analysis, I'll now present the relevant code locations for implementing `assert_consistent_docs()`:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 451\nfunction: check_consistent_length\n\nsklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\nline: 3281\nfunction: check_no_attributes_set_in_init\n\nsklearn/utils/_param_validation.py\nline: 826\nfunction: generate_valid_param\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 151\nclass: BaseEstimator\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 1510\nfunction: has_fit_parameter\n```\n\nThis localization is based on:\n\n1. The core functionality would be implemented in `sklearn/utils/validation.py` as it already contains similar validation functions (`check_consistent_length`, `has_fit_parameter`) and follows the same pattern for docstring validation.\n\n2. The feature would need to integrate with existing validation infrastructure, particularly the estimator checks in `estimator_checks.py` and parameter validation in `_param_validation.py`.\n\n3. The implementation would need to use BaseEstimator's parameter system for tracking parameters.\n\n4. The function would need to handle both parameter, attribute, and return sections of docstrings consistently.\n\nThese locations are chosen because:\n- They are part of the validation framework where similar features reside\n- They contain the infrastructure for parameter and attribute handling\n- They demonstrate the pattern for docstring validation\n- They provide the necessary utilities for processing docstrings"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "2cbf3345bce979b261f7007616cc3465f33a51d3", "problem_statement": "[WIP] : Added assert_consistent_docs() and related tests\n\r\nFixes #9388 \r\n\r\nAdded a function to check for consistency between docstring of objects.\r\n\r\nIn this approach there is a python dictionary for each of Parameters, Attributes and Returns. Indexed by the name of the parameter/attribute/return with the value being a Python dictionary containing its type definition and description. The function checks for each object if the docstring is identical (excluding whitespaces) for a given parameter/attribute/return in the main dictionary. If a new parameter/attribute/return are found they are added in the main dictionary.\n", "patch": "diff --git a/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py b/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py\nindex 0175966a21b74..b69855633a730 100644\n--- a/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py\n+++ b/sklearn/metrics/_classification.py\n@@ -1727,14 +1727,18 @@ def precision_recall_fscore_support(\n         \"assigned\" 0 samples. For multilabel targets, labels are column indices.\n         By default, all labels in `y_true` and `y_pred` are used in sorted order.\n \n+        .. versionchanged:: 0.17\n+           Parameter `labels` improved for multiclass problem.\n+\n     pos_label : int, float, bool or str, default=1\n         The class to report if `average='binary'` and the data is binary,\n         otherwise this parameter is ignored.\n         For multiclass or multilabel targets, set `labels=[pos_label]` and\n         `average != 'binary'` to report metrics for one label only.\n \n-    average : {'binary', 'micro', 'macro', 'samples', 'weighted'}, \\\n-            default=None\n+    average : {'micro', 'macro', 'samples', 'weighted', 'binary'} or None, \\\n+            default='binary'\n+        This parameter is required for multiclass/multilabel targets.\n         If ``None``, the metrics for each class are returned. Otherwise, this\n         determines the type of averaging performed on the data:\n \n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 71809, "completion_tokens": 470}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must