ed from scipy `tools/gh_lists.py` and `tools/authors.py`.\n 26 | \n 27 | Examples\n 28 | --------\n 29 | \n 30 | From the bash command line with $GITHUB token.\n 31 | \n 32 |     $ ./scripts/announce.py $GITHUB v1.11.0..v1.11.1 > announce.rst\n 33 | \n 34 | \"\"\"\n 35 | \n 36 | import codecs\n 37 | import os\n 38 | import re\n 39 | import textwrap\n 40 | \n 41 | from git import Repo\n 42 | \n 43 | # Contributors to be renamed.\n 44 | CONTRIBUTOR_MAPPING = {\"znkjnffrezna\": \"znetbgcubravk\"}\n 45 | \n 46 | UTF8Writer = codecs.getwriter(\"utf8\")\n 47 | this_repo = Repo(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(__file__), \"..\", \"..\"))\n 48 | \n 49 | author_msg = \"\"\"\\\n 50 | A total of %d people contributed patches to this release.  People with a\n 51 | \"+\" by their names contributed a patch for the first time.\n 52 | \"\"\"\n 53 | \n 54 | pull_request_msg = \"\"\"\\\n 55 | A total of %d pull requests were merged for this release.\n 56 | \"\"\"\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/io/pytables.py:WORMTable.read`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n4405 | def read(\n4406 |         self,\n4407 |         where=None,\n4408 |         columns=None,\n4409 |         start: int | None = None,\n4410 |         stop: int | None = None,\n4411 |     ):\n4412 |         \"\"\"\n4413 |         read the indices and the indexing array, calculate offset rows and return\n4414 |         \"\"\"\n4415 |         raise NotImplementedError(\"WORMTable needs to implement read\")\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"CONTRIBUTING\", \"PandasDEVELOPER CONTRIBUTING\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.transpose`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n3646 | def transpose(\n3647 |         self,\n3648 |         *args,\n3649 |         copy: bool | lib.NoDefault = lib.no_default,\n3650 |     ) -> DataFrame:\n3651 |         \"\"\"\n3652 |         Transpose index and columns.\n3653 | \n3654 |         Reflect the DataFrame over its main diagonal by writing rows as columns\n3655 |         and vice-versa. The property :attr:`.T` is an accessor to the method\n3656 |         :meth:`transpose`.\n3657 | \n3658 |         Parameters\n3659 |         ----------\n3660 |         *args : tuple, optional\n3661 |             Accepted for compatibility with NumPy.\n3662 |         copy : bool, default False\n3663 |             Whether to copy the data after transposing, even for DataFrames\n3664 |             with a single dtype.\n3665 | \n3666 |             Note that a copy is always required for mixed dtype DataFrames,\n3667 |             or for DataFrames with any extension types.\n3668 | \n3669 |             .. note::\n3670 |                 The `copy` keyword will change behavior in pandas 3.0.\n3671 |                 `Copy-on-Write\n3672 |                 <https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/dev/user_guide/copy_on_write.html>`__\n3673 |                 will be enabled by default, which means that all methods with a\n3674 |                 `copy` keyword will use a lazy copy mechanism to defer the copy and\n3675 |                 ignore the `copy` keyword. The `copy` keyword will be removed in a\n3676 |                 future version of pandas.\n3677 | \n3678 |                 You can already get the future behavior and improvements through\n3679 |                 enabling copy on write ``pd.options.mode.copy_on_write = True``\n3680 | \n3681 |             .. deprecated:: 3.0.0\n3682 | \n3683 |         Returns\n3684 |         -------\n3685 |         DataFrame\n3686 |             The transposed DataFrame.\n3687 | \n3688 |         See Also\n3689 |         --------\n3690 |         numpy.transpose : Permute the dimensions of a given array.\n3691 | \n3692 |         Notes\n3693 |         -----\n3694 |         Transposing a DataFrame with mixed dtypes will result in a homogeneous\n3695 |         DataFrame with the `object` dtype. In such a case, a copy of the data\n3696 |         is always made.\n3697 | \n3698 |         Examples\n3699 |         --------\n3700 |         **Square DataFrame with homogeneous dtype**\n3701 | \n3702 |         >>> d1 = {\"col1\": [1, 2], \"col2\": [3, 4]}\n3703 |         >>> df1 = pd.DataFrame(data=d1)\n3704 |         >>> df1\n3705 |            col1  col2\n3706 |         0     1     3\n3707 |         1     2     4\n3708 | \n3709 |         >>> df1_transposed = df1.T  # or df1.transpose()\n3710 |         >>> df1_transposed\n3711 |               0  1\n3712 |         col1  1  2\n3713 |         col2  3  4\n3714 | \n3715 |         When the dtype is homogeneous in the original DataFrame, we get a\n3716 |         transposed DataFrame with the same dtype:\n3717 | \n3718 |         >>> df1.dtypes\n3719 |         col1    int64\n3720 |         col2    int64\n3721 |         dtype: object\n3722 |         >>> df1_transposed.dtypes\n3723 |         0    int64\n3724 |         1    int64\n3725 |         dtype: object\n3726 | \n3727 |         **Non-square DataFrame with mixed dtypes**\n3728 | \n3729 |         >>> d2 = {\n3730 |         ...     \"name\": [\"Alice\", \"Bob\"],\n3731 |         ...     \"score\": [9.5, 8],\n3732 |         ...     \"employed\": [False, True],\n3733 |         ...     \"kids\": [0, 0],\n3734 |         ... }\n3735 |         >>> df2 = pd.DataFrame(data=d2)\n3736 |         >>> df2\n3737 |             name  score  employed  kids\n3738 |         0  Alice    9.5     False     0\n3739 |         1    Bob    8.0      True     0\n3740 | \n3741 |         >>> df2_transposed = df2.T  # or df2.transpose()\n3742 |         >>> df2_transposed\n3743 |                       0     1\n3744 |         name      Alice   Bob\n3745 |         score       9.5   8.0\n3746 |         employed  False  True\n3747 |         kids          0     0\n3748 | \n3749 |         When the DataFrame has mixed dtypes, we get a transposed DataFrame with\n3750 |         the `object` dtype:\n3751 | \n3752 |         >>> df2.dtypes\n3753 |         name         object\n3754 |         score  