7 | \n28 | # scatter plot (Note: `plt.scatter` doesn't use default colors)\n29 | x, y = np.random.normal(size=(2, 200))\n30 | ax1.plot(x, y, 'o')\n31 | \n32 | # sinusoidal lines with colors from default color cycle\n33 | L = 2*np.pi\n34 | x = np.linspace(0, L)\n35 | ncolors = len(plt.rcParams['axes.prop_cycle'])\n36 | shift = np.linspace(0, L, ncolors, endpoint=False)\n37 | for s in shift:\n38 |     ax2.plot(x, np.sin(x + s), '-')\n39 | ax2.margins(0)\n40 | \n41 | # bar graphs\n42 | x = np.arange(5)\n43 | y1, y2 = np.random.randint(1, 25, size=(2, 5))\n44 | width = 0.25\n45 | ax3.bar(x, y1, width)\n46 | ax3.bar(x + width, y2, width,\n47 |         color=list(plt.rcParams['axes.prop_cycle'])[2]['color'])\n48 | ax3.set_xticks(x + width, labels=['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'])\n49 | \n50 | # circles with colors from default color cycle\n51 | for i, color in enumerate(plt.rcParams['axes.prop_cycle']):\n52 |     xy = np.random.normal(size=2)\n53 |     ax4.add_patch(plt.Circle(xy, radius=0.3, color=color['color']))\n54 | ax4.axis('equal')\n55 | ax4.margins(0)\n56 | \n57 | plt.show()\n58 | \n```\n\nFound code snippet in file `galleries/examples/axisartist/demo_floating_axes.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | ==========================\n  3 | ``floating_axes`` features\n  4 | ==========================\n  5 | \n  6 | Demonstration of features of the :mod:`.floating_axes` module:\n  7 | \n  8 | * Using `~.axes.Axes.scatter` and `~.axes.Axes.bar` with changing the shape of\n  9 |   the plot.\n 10 | * Using `~.floating_axes.GridHelperCurveLinear` to rotate the plot and set the\n 11 |   plot boundary.\n 12 | * Using `~.Figure.add_subplot` to create a subplot using the return value from\n 13 |   `~.floating_axes.GridHelperCurveLinear`.\n 14 | * Making a sector plot by adding more features to\n 15 |   `~.floating_axes.GridHelperCurveLinear`.\n 16 | \"\"\"\n 17 | \n 18 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 19 | import numpy as np\n 2