r', 'higher', 'midpoint', 'nearest'}\n13137 |             This optional parameter specifies the interpolation method to use,\n13138 |             when the desired quantile lies between two data points `i` and `j`:\n13139 | \n13140 |             * linear: `i + (j - i) * fraction`, where `fraction` is the\n13141 |               fractional part of the index surrounded by `i` and `j`.\n13142 |             * lower: `i`.\n13143 |             * higher: `j`.\n13144 |             * nearest: `i` or `j` whichever is nearest.\n13145 |             * midpoint: (`i` + `j`) / 2.\n13146 |         method : {'single', 'table'}, default 'single'\n13147 |             Whether to compute quantiles per-column ('single') or over all columns\n13148 |             ('table'). When 'table', the only allowed interpolation methods are\n13149 |             'nearest', 'lower', and 'higher'.\n13150 | \n13151 |         Returns\n13152 |         -------\n13153 |         Series or DataFrame\n13154 | \n13155 |             If ``q`` is an array, a DataFrame will be returned where the\n13156 |               index is ``q``, the columns are the columns of self, and the\n13157 |               values are the quantiles.\n13158 |             If ``q`` is a float, a Series will be returned where the\n13159 |               index is the columns of self and the values are the quantiles.\n13160 | \n13161 |         See Also\n13162 |         --------\n13163 |         core.window.rolling.Rolling.quantile: Rolling quantile.\n13164 |         numpy.percentile: Numpy function to compute the percentile.\n13165 | \n13166 |         Examples\n13167 |         --------\n13168 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(\n13169 |         ...     np.array([[1, 1], [2, 10], [3, 100], [4, 100]]), columns=[\"a\", \"b\"]\n13170 |         ... )\n13171 |         >>> df.quantile(0.1)\n13172 |         a    1.3\n13173 |         b    3.7\n13174 |         Name: 0.1, dtype: float64\n13175 |         >>> df.quantile([0.1, 0.5])\n13176 |                a     b\